; AX3031, every way a format string can be malformed, and one case
; per way. The point of the code is that all of them are found at
; EXPANSION time - before the checker, and long before the program
; runs - so none of them can be a runtime formatting failure. There is
; no runtime format parser to fail in.
;
; Each caret has to land INSIDE the string literal, on the byte at
; fault, and not on the invocation: a format string is a small
; language embedded in a literal, and a diagnostic that points at the
; whole call is no better than no diagnostic. The `.human` golden is
; what pins that.
;
; Not here, because they are not this code:
;   {nope}   an unbound hole is AX3001, at the hole's own span
;   {c}      a type with no `Show` instance is AX3025
;   {s:.2}   a well-formed specifier applied to the wrong type is
;            AX3004, on `fmtFloatPrec`'s Float parameter
; Those three are the division of labour this design turns on - the
; expander owns the STRING's shape, the checker owns the VALUE's type
; - and each is asserted where it belongs.
(import IO)

(:: unclosed (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (unclosed n) (println "a {n"))

(:: stray (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (stray n) (println "a } b"))

(:: empty (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (empty n) (println "a {} b"))

(:: notAName (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (notAName n) (println "a {1n} b"))

(:: badByteAfterName (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (badByteAfterName n) (println "a {n b"))

(:: unclosedWithSpec (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (unclosedWithSpec n) (println "a {n:>8"))

(:: badSpecType (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (badSpecType n) (println "a {n:q} b"))

(:: precisionWithoutDigits (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (precisionWithoutDigits n) (println "a {n:.} b"))

; The precision written AFTER the type instead of before it - the one
; specifier mistake whose parts are each individually valid.
(:: specTrailing (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (specTrailing n) (println "a {n:8x.2} b"))

; The two that were not refusals but CRASHES until the accumulators
; were capped: an unbounded width wrapped and the program trapped in
; the allocator at exit 70 with no diagnostic at all, and the same
; digits as a precision made expansion hang. Both are compile-time
; refusals naming the limit they enforced.
(:: widthOverflow (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (widthOverflow n) (println "a {n:99999999999999999999} b"))

(:: precisionOverflow (-> Float Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (precisionOverflow x) (println "a {x:.99999999999999999999} b"))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) 0)
